# Paradigm Networks login check

This plugin (Paradigm Networks) gates every prompt and tool call through
Paradigm Networks, which requires this workspace to be logged in. Two other
mechanisms try to surface a missing login, but **neither reliably reaches
you (the agent) on the first message of a session**, so this rule is the
backstop:

- `check-session.sh` (`sessionStart`) injects context asking you to request the
  base URL, but `sessionStart` is fire-and-forget — the agent loop doesn't wait
  for or enforce it, so it can race with (or miss) the user's first message.
- `check-prompt.sh` (`beforeSubmitPrompt`) only supports `continue` /
  `user_message` output fields. `user_message` is a UI-level notice to the
  human when a prompt is blocked — it is never injected into your context, so
  an "allowed" prompt with Paradigm Networks unconfigured gives you no signal
  to act on.

## What to do

On the **first user message of a session**, before doing anything else, check
whether Paradigm Networks is already configured. Do this with a plain shell
command — do **not** try to locate this plugin's own `scripts/` directory to
import `pn_config.sh`; the plugin can be installed anywhere
(`~/.cursor/plugins/...`) and there is no environment variable that tells you
where, so path-guessing is unreliable. The credentials file lives at a fixed,
well-known path regardless of install location. macOS/Linux:

```bash
if [ -f ~/.pn/credentials.json ] || { [ -n "$PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL" ] && [ -n "$PARADIGM_NETWORKS_TOKEN" ]; } || { [ -n "$SNANTIZER_BASE_URL" ] && [ -n "$SNANTIZER_TOKEN" ]; }; then
  echo CONFIGURED
elif [ -n "$PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL" ]; then
  echo NOT_CONFIGURED_URL_KNOWN
else
  echo NOT_CONFIGURED
fi
```

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
if ((Test-Path "$HOME\.pn\credentials.json") -or ($env:PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL -and $env:PARADIGM_NETWORKS_TOKEN) -or ($env:SNANTIZER_BASE_URL -and $env:SNANTIZER_TOKEN)) {
  Write-Output "CONFIGURED"
} elseif ($env:PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL) {
  Write-Output "NOT_CONFIGURED_URL_KNOWN"
} else {
  Write-Output "NOT_CONFIGURED"
}
```

- `CONFIGURED` → Paradigm Networks already has a stored login (or a
  shared-host env override is set). Proceed with the user's request normally
  — no further action needed. (This only checks presence, not validity — an
  expired token that fails to refresh is already handled by
  `check-prompt.sh`/`check-write.sh` failing open/closed on their own, so
  don't try to validate the token here.)
- `NOT_CONFIGURED_URL_KNOWN` → an admin has already set
  `PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL` for this workspace (e.g. via the plugin's
  marketplace configuration), but no one has completed login yet. Do **not**
  ask for the base URL — run the `paradigmnetworks-login` skill directly using
  `$PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL` as the known value, skipping straight to its
  login step, before proceeding with the user's request.
- `NOT_CONFIGURED` → Paradigm Networks is **not** configured and no base URL
  is known. You must invoke the `AskQuestion` tool itself to ask for the base
  URL — actually call the tool, do not just type the prompt and options into
  a chat message as text or a bulleted list. Writing it out in prose produces
  none of the clickable UI or free-text "Other" field; it just looks like a
  plain question to the user, which is exactly what this is meant to avoid.
  Call it with:
  - prompt: "What's your Paradigm Networks base URL? (e.g. `https://acme.paradigmnetworks.ai`). Don't have one yet? Sign up at https://signup.claude-demo.paradigmnetworks.ai/signup."
  - options: `"I'm not sure what my base URL is"`, `"I think I'm already logged in — recheck"`
  - the user's own URL comes back via the automatic "Other" option; do not
    invent, guess, or reuse a base URL from another project or example.
    Validate it once, correct at most one mistake, then move on — see the
    `paradigmnetworks-login` skill for the full flow, including what to do if it still
    doesn't look like a URL after that, and for what to do if they pick
    "I'm not sure what my base URL is" (point them to signup, don't guess).

  Once you have it, run the `paradigmnetworks-login` skill to log in before proceeding
  with their original request.

This automatic use of `$PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL` only applies to this passive,
first-message check. If the user explicitly asks to log in, switch Paradigm
Networks orgs, or gives their own URL directly, that always takes priority —
run the `paradigmnetworks-login` skill with whatever URL they actually gave you, regardless
of what `PARADIGM_NETWORKS_URL` is set to.

Do not treat an allowed prompt or a "Paradigm Networks unreachable" message
as proof that Paradigm Networks is configured — `check-prompt.sh` and
`check-write.sh` fail open on purpose, so prompts and tool calls can proceed
even when there's no login at all.

You only need to do this once per session — after you've confirmed Paradigm
Networks is configured (or completed a login), don't repeat the check on
later messages unless the user asks to log in again or a hook message
reports the login expired.
